Subject: [PATCH] usb: hub: run the hub workqueue at high priority
Upstream-Status: Pending

With the two previous patches the hub thread is on the port 0.4ms
after the connect, but under boot load it still took 113ms to assert
the reset and 212ms from reset to the first descriptor where an idle
system takes 73ms. The role switch on a NAC Wave 2 happens ~1s after
boot, alongside the aic8800 firmware upload with its md5 pass, RF
calibration and the readahead sweep, on one core. The head unit waits
~200ms from its own pull-up; that attempt was at ~185ms and succeeded
with the margin of a coin toss.

Enumeration is latency-bound work of a few hundred microseconds per
step, interleaved with waits. Run hub_wq as a WQ_HIGHPRI workqueue so
its worker gets the CPU ahead of the bulk work whenever it is
runnable. No behavioural change beyond scheduling priority.

Optional: the handshake works without it on an idle system; this is
margin for the loaded case.

---
 drivers/usb/core/hub.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
@@ -6109,7 +6109,15 @@
 	 * device was gone before the EHCI controller had handed its port
 	 * over to the companion full-speed controller.
 	 */
-	hub_wq = alloc_workqueue("usb_hub_wq", WQ_FREEZABLE, 0);
+	/*
+	 * WQ_HIGHPRI: on a single-core SoC the hub thread competes with
+	 * WiFi firmware upload, RF calibration and readahead right when a
+	 * head unit swaps roles and waits ~200ms for its bus reset. Measured
+	 * on a NAC Wave 2: 113ms from port connect to the reset being
+	 * asserted under boot load, ~10ms when idle. Enumeration is
+	 * latency-bound, not throughput-bound; let it win the CPU.
+	 */
+	hub_wq = alloc_workqueue("usb_hub_wq", WQ_FREEZABLE | WQ_HIGHPRI, 0);
 	if (hub_wq)
 		return 0;
 
